ML_PAGE("l7-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l7\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 7 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 7 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 28 multiple-choice questions (4 of them coding questions) sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them. Every question also has a <b>harder version</b> on the same concept, one reveal below its answer.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>28</strong><span>MCQs in the notes, each with a harder version</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>4</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Bloom challenge questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l7\" data-quiz=\"7\">Start Lecture 7 simulator</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"hard\">Hard mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Bloom challenge</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l7\" id=\"l7p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">10 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">LECTURE 7 - SVM, Naïve Bayes, KNN and Artificial Neural Networks</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s1\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Know instantly</button></li><li data-full=\"Part A - Support Vector Machine (SVM)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s2\" title=\"Part A - Support Vector Machine (SVM)\">SVM</button></li><li data-full=\"Part B - Naïve Bayes\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s3\" title=\"Part B - Naïve Bayes\">Naive Bayes</button></li><li data-full=\"Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s4\" title=\"Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN)\">KNN</button></li><li data-full=\"Part D - Artificial Neural Networks\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s5\" title=\"Part D - Artificial Neural Networks\">Neural networks</button></li><li data-full=\"Part E - Lecture 7 Coding Questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s6\" title=\"Part E - Lecture 7 Coding Questions\">Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Lecture 7 - Answer Key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s7\" title=\"Lecture 7 - Answer Key\">Answer key</button></li><li data-full=\"The biggest MCQ traps in Lectures 6-7\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s8\" title=\"The biggest MCQ traps in Lectures 6-7\">Eight exam traps</button></li><li data-full=\"Final Rapid-Fire Revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s9\" title=\"Final Rapid-Fire Revision\">Rapid-fire revision</button></li><li data-full=\"What I would prioritize before the exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l7p-s10\" title=\"What I would prioritize before the exam\">What to prioritise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l7\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 7 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions - section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>10 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l7p-d0\">LECTURE 7 - SVM, Naïve Bayes, KNN and Artificial Neural Networks</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Immediate answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM</td>\n<td>Finds a separating hyperplane</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum margin</td>\n<td>Maximizes separation from nearby observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Support vectors</td>\n<td>Critical points determining the boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVM parameter C</td>\n<td>Controls penalty for margin violations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linear kernel</td>\n<td>Linear separation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>RBF kernel</td>\n<td>Nonlinear decision boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n<td>Probabilistic classification using Bayes' theorem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prior</td>\n<td>Probability before observing evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Likelihood</td>\n<td>Probability of evidence given a class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Posterior</td>\n<td>Updated class probability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naïve assumption</td>\n<td>Conditional independence of features given class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Laplace smoothing</td>\n<td>Addresses zero-frequency estimates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN</td>\n<td>Classifies using nearby observations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K</td>\n<td>Number of neighbours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lazy learning</td>\n<td>Defers most computation until prediction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANN</td>\n<td>Interconnected weighted computational neurons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bias</td>\n<td>Additional adjustable term in neuron computation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Activation function</td>\n<td>Introduces nonlinear transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReLU</td>\n<td>$\\max(0,z)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sigmoid</td>\n<td>Output between 0 and 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backpropagation</td>\n<td>Calculates gradients through the network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning rate</td>\n<td>Controls update step size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XOR</td>\n<td>Not linearly separable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early stopping</td>\n<td>Stops training based on validation performance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part A - Support Vector Machine (SVM)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>What is the primary objective of a linear SVM classifier?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Minimize the total number of training observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Find a separating hyperplane with a large margin</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Estimate the mean target value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Group observations without labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H25</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Two straight lines both separate the training data perfectly. Line 1 passes 0.2 units from the nearest point of each class; line 2 passes 1.5 units from the nearest points. Which would a linear SVM choose, and why?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Line 1 - it hugs the data more closely, so it is more precise.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Either - both have zero training error, so SVM has no preference.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Line 2 - among separating boundaries, SVM looks for the one with the largest margin.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Line 1 - it has fewer support vectors.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>SVM's objective is not just \"separate\" but \"separate with the <strong>largest margin</strong>\", so it prefers line 2. A confuses closeness with quality. B is the trap: zero training error does not decide between them for SVM. D: the number of support vectors is not the criterion.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>Why does SVM attempt to maximize the margin?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To increase the number of input features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To create probabilities for every possible class</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To obtain a separating boundary with greater tolerance to nearby variations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To remove all training errors regardless of the data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H26</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A new customer's data lands slightly away from the training cluster of their class, close to the boundary. How does a large margin help with this customer?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Small shifts or noise in new points are less likely to push them across the boundary, so the classifier tolerates nearby variation.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The large margin produces calibrated probabilities for the customer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A large margin guarantees zero test error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A large margin adds new features describing the customer.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The margin is a <strong>buffer</strong>: points can vary a little and still fall on the correct side. B: SVM outputs a decision, not calibrated probabilities. C over-promises. D is unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>What are support vectors?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The observations that directly influence the position of the SVM boundary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Randomly generated synthetic observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Features with the largest numerical values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The final predicted class labels</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H27</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>After training a linear SVM, you delete 50 training points that lie far from the margin and retrain. Separately, you delete one support vector and retrain. What happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both deletions move the boundary by the same amount.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Deleting the 50 far points moves the boundary most, because there are more of them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Neither moves the boundary, because SVM depends only on the class means.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Deleting the far points leaves the boundary unchanged, but deleting a support vector can move it - only the support vectors constrain the boundary.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Support vectors are the points <strong>on or inside the margin</strong> that determine the decision function; points far from it play no part. B counts points instead of asking which ones constrain the boundary. C describes a centroid method, not SVM. A ignores the difference.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>In soft-margin SVM, what is the general effect of increasing parameter $C$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reduce the penalty for margin violations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Make the model automatically unsupervised</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Replace the kernel with a Decision Tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Increase the penalty for margin violations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H28</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A dataset has a few mislabelled points near the boundary. A soft-margin SVM is trained with $C=0.01$ and with $C=100$. Which description of the $C=100$ model is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A wider margin with more violations allowed - it is more regularized.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It penalizes violations heavily and bends to fit the training points, giving a narrower margin that can overfit the mislabelled points.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It becomes unsupervised.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is identical to the $C=0.01$ model - C only affects speed.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Large C = large penalty for margin violations</strong>, so the model tries hard to classify every training point, including noise. A describes <strong>small</strong> C. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>A dataset contains two classes arranged in concentric circles, making straight-line separation impossible.</p>\n<p>Which is an appropriate SVM approach to consider?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Linear SVM without feature transformations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SVM with an RBF kernel</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ordinary Least Squares Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Naïve Bayes without any input features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H29</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>In a concentric-circles dataset, the inner class has radius below 1 and the outer ring has radius about 2. You add a new feature $z=x^2+y^2$. What becomes possible?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - the classes still overlap in three dimensions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A straight line in the original two-dimensional plane now separates them.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A flat plane such as $z=2$ separates them: inner points have $z&lt;1$ and ring points $z\\approx4$, which corresponds to a circular boundary in the original space.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only a decision tree can use the new feature.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Mapping to a <strong>higher-dimensional space</strong> where a hyperplane separates the classes is the idea behind kernels; the RBF kernel achieves a similar effect without building the features explicitly. A ignores the new feature. B: in the original space the boundary is still a circle. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>Why is feature scaling generally important for SVM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SVM can only accept features between 0 and 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SVM requires every feature to be categorical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature magnitudes can influence the geometry, margins and kernel-based similarity calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scaling guarantees perfect classification</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H30</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>An RBF-kernel SVM uses income (RM) and age, unscaled. The kernel depends on $\\lVert x-x'\\rVert^2$. What happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing - kernels are unaffected by scale.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SVC refuses inputs outside [0, 1].</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Age dominates, because its values are smaller.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Income differences dominate the squared distance, so similarity is decided almost entirely by income; the features should be scaled first.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The RBF kernel is <strong>distance-based</strong>, so the same dominance problem as KNN appears. A is the misconception the lecture warns about. B is false. C has it backwards - large-magnitude features dominate.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The SVM slides emphasize hyperplanes, maximum margins, support vectors, soft-margin $C$, and nonlinear kernels.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part B - Naïve Bayes</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>Bayes' Theorem is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(C\\mid X)=\\frac{P(X\\mid C)P(C)}{P(X)}$$</div>\n<p>What does $P(C\\mid X)$ represent?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The prior probability of C</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The posterior probability of C after observing X</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The probability of evidence X alone</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The total number of features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H31</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>In a spam filter, $P(\\text{\"free\"}\\mid Spam)=0.40$. A colleague concludes: \"an email containing <em>free</em> is spam with probability 0.40\". What is wrong?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They have confused the likelihood $P(X\\mid C)$ with the posterior $P(C\\mid X)$; the posterior also needs the prior $P(Spam)$ and the evidence $P(X)$.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Nothing - the two probabilities are the same.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.40 is the prior probability of spam.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The posterior is $1-0.40=0.60$.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Bayes' Theorem turns $P(X\\mid C)$ into $P(C\\mid X)$ only by <strong>multiplying by the prior and dividing by the evidence</strong>. B is the classic confusion. C mislabels the term. D is not a valid calculation.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>A dataset has 10 customers:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">6 customers → Buy = Yes\n4 customers → Buy = No\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the prior probability of Buy = Yes?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.8</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Prior probability</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes)=\\frac6{10}=\\boxed{0.6}$$</div>\n<p>The prior is calculated before observing the new customer's evidence.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H32</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>In a training set of 200 emails, 30 are spam. What is $P(Spam)$, and how does Naïve Bayes use it?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.30 - it is multiplied by each feature separately.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.15 - it multiplies the product of the spam class's likelihoods.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.85 - it is added to the spam score.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.5 - classes are treated as equally likely until a word is seen.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$30/200=0.15$. The class score is $P(C)\\prod_i P(X_i\\mid C)$, so the <strong>prior</strong> appears once as a factor. A miscalculates and misplaces it. C is $P(Not\\ spam)$, and scores multiply rather than add. D ignores the class frequencies in the training data.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>Why is Naïve Bayes called \"naïve\"?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It assumes features are conditionally independent given the target class</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It cannot use probability calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It requires every feature to be identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It uses distances between observations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H33</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A Naïve Bayes spam filter uses both \"contains <em>free</em>\" and \"contains <em>free gift</em>\" as features. Why is this a problem for Naïve Bayes specifically?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Naïve Bayes cannot use binary features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Naïve Bayes needs numeric features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Naïve Bayes automatically ignores duplicated features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The two features are strongly dependent, but Naïve Bayes treats them as conditionally independent and multiplies both likelihoods, counting much the same evidence twice and making its probabilities overconfident.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The <strong>conditional-independence</strong> assumption is what makes the method \"naïve\"; when it is badly broken, the product double-counts evidence - one reason the lecture says the probabilities can be poorly calibrated. A, B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>A Naïve Bayes model has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes)=0.6,\\quad P(X\\mid Yes)=0.3$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(No)=0.4,\\quad P(X\\mid No)=0.2$$</div>\n<p>What is the normalized posterior probability $P(Yes\\mid X)$, rounded to three decimals?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.180</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.400</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.600</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.692</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p><strong>Working - Naïve Bayes posterior</strong></p>\n<p>First calculate the unnormalized class scores:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(Yes)=0.6(0.3)=0.18$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(No)=0.4(0.2)=0.08$$</div>\n<p>Normalize:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes\\mid X)=\\frac{0.18}{0.18+0.08}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{0.18}{0.26}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P(Yes\\mid X)\\approx0.692}$$</div>\n<p>Since Yes has the higher posterior, predict Yes.</p>\n<p>Exam trap: The raw value 0.18 is not the normalized posterior probability.</p></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H34</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A Naïve Bayes model has $P(Yes)=0.3$, $P(X\\mid Yes)=0.5$, $P(No)=0.7$ and $P(X\\mid No)=0.1$. What is $P(Yes\\mid X)$?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.15</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.682 - Yes wins even though its prior is smaller</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.318</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Scores are $0.3\\times0.5=0.15$ and $0.7\\times0.1=0.07$; normalizing gives $0.15/0.22\\approx0.682$. Strong evidence can <strong>overturn the prior</strong>. A is the unnormalized score. B is the prior. D is $P(No\\mid X)$.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>What is the zero-frequency problem in Naïve Bayes?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A feature is standardized to zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> An unseen feature value can give a class likelihood of zero, making the product zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The dataset contains no target labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The classifier has exactly zero parameters</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H35</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>No Buy = Yes customer in training had Income = Low. A new customer has Income = Low, but every other feature strongly matches Buy = Yes. Without smoothing, what happens?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Score(Yes) = 0 whatever the other features say, so the model predicts No - one unseen value vetoes the class.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The Low income value is ignored.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Naïve Bayes raises an error.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Score(Yes) is reduced slightly.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The class score is a <strong>product</strong>, and $P(Low\\mid Yes)=0/6=0$ makes the whole product zero. B and D underestimate the effect. C is false - the model quietly returns a skewed answer, which is why this problem is dangerous.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q36</span><div><p>Which technique is commonly used to address the zero-frequency problem?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Polynomial Regression</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> MinMaxScaler</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Laplace smoothing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Random Forest voting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H36</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>The Buy = Yes class has 6 customers. Income has $K=3$ values, with counts Low 0, Medium 2, High 4. What are the add-one (Laplace) estimates of $P(Income\\mid Yes)$?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0, 1/3 and 2/3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1/6, 3/6 and 5/6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1/7, 3/7 and 5/7</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1/9, 3/9 and 5/9 - they still sum to 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$\\frac{Count(v,C)+1}{Count(C)+K}=\\frac{0+1}{9},\\frac{2+1}{9},\\frac{4+1}{9}$. Adding $K$ to the denominator keeps the estimates a valid distribution. A is the unsmoothed estimate. B forgets $+K$ (the values sum to 1.5). C adds only 1 to the denominator.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The lecture introduces Bayes' Theorem, class-conditional independence and the zero-frequency problem before its customer-buying example.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part C - K-Nearest Neighbours (KNN)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l7_knn_k\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Choosing K in KNN</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · KNN decision regions for small and large K, with leave-one-out accuracy - and what happens when the features are not scaled.</figcaption></figure></div><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q37</span><div><p>Why is KNN described as a lazy learning algorithm?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It performs no predictions during testing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It generally stores training examples and performs much of the computation during prediction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It always requires neural networks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It trains multiple decision trees</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H37</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Compared with logistic regression, where does KNN's computational cost fall?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN's training is slow, but each prediction is instant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KNN's training is almost free (it stores the data), but each prediction computes distances to the stored observations, so prediction slows as the training set grows.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The cost is the same as logistic regression's.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KNN discards the training data after fitting.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Lazy learning</strong> defers the work to prediction time; logistic regression does the opposite (expensive fit, cheap prediction). A reverses it. C is false. D: KNN needs the stored examples to predict.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q38</span><div><p>A KNN classifier uses:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$K=3$$</div>\n<p>The three nearest neighbours have labels:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Yes\nYes\nNo\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is the predicted class using majority voting?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Unknown</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both classes equally</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H38</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>With $K=5$, the neighbours' labels are Yes, Yes, No, No, No, and the two Yes neighbours are much closer than the three No neighbours. What does a standard (unweighted) KNN predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes, because the closest neighbours decide</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A tie</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> No - each of the K neighbours gets one equal vote (3 No vs 2 Yes), however close it is</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Yes, because K is odd</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Plain majority voting counts <strong>each neighbour once</strong>; distance only decides who is in the top K. A describes a distance-weighted variant. B: 3 vs 2 is not a tie. D: an odd K prevents a binary tie but does not choose the class.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q39</span><div><p>Calculate the Euclidean distance between:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A=(0,0),\\quad B=(3,4)$$</div></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 7</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Euclidean distance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{(3-0)^2+(4-0)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{9+16}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{25}=\\boxed{5}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H39</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>What is the Euclidean distance between $A=(1,2,3)$ and $B=(4,6,15)$?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 13</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 19</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 169</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 12</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Differences 3, 4, 12; $\\sqrt{9+16+144}=\\sqrt{169}=13$. B adds the absolute differences (Manhattan distance). C forgets the square root. D takes only the largest difference.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q40</span><div><p>What is a possible problem with using:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$K=1$$</div>\n<p>in KNN?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The algorithm cannot classify binary labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The prediction is always the overall majority class</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model cannot use distances</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The prediction may be overly sensitive to noisy individual observations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H40</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A transaction mislabelled as Fraud sits inside a dense cluster of legitimate transactions. A new legitimate transaction lands right next to it. What do $K=1$ and $K=5$ predict?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both predict Fraud.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $K=1$ predicts Fraud by copying the single noisy neighbour; $K=5$ most likely predicts Legitimate, because the other neighbours outvote it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $K=1$ predicts Legitimate and $K=5$ predicts Fraud.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both predict Legitimate, because KNN removes noisy points.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>With $K=1$ the prediction depends on <strong>one observation</strong>, so a single noisy label decides it. A larger K averages over a neighbourhood. C reverses the effect. D: KNN keeps every stored point, mislabelled or not.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q41</span><div><p>What is a possible consequence of selecting an excessively large K?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The model automatically becomes more complex</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Local patterns may be smoothed away, causing underfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The algorithm becomes a Decision Tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every neighbour receives a new target label</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H41</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A training set has 100 points: 70 Class A and 30 Class B. What does KNN predict with $K=100$?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The most complex possible decision boundary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predictions that follow local patterns most closely</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model turns into a decision tree</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Class A for every new point - the overall majority - so all local structure is lost</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>When K equals the training-set size, every query has the <strong>same neighbours</strong>, so the prediction is always the majority class: extreme underfitting. A and B describe small K. C is false.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q42</span><div><p>A KNN model uses Age (20-70) and Annual Income (20,000-500,000).</p>\n<p>What is the main preprocessing concern?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Income may dominate distance calculations because of its numerical scale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Age must be removed because it is numerical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KNN always requires one-hot encoding of every numerical feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Feature scaling makes the target labels disappear</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H42</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A query customer is (age 30, income 50,000). Neighbour P is (31, 53,000) and neighbour Q is (60, 50,500). Ages range 20-70 and incomes 20,000-500,000. Which neighbour is nearest, before and after Min-Max scaling?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P in both cases</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Q in both cases</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Q before scaling (distance ≈ 501 vs ≈ 3,000, because income dominates), but P after scaling (≈ 0.02 vs ≈ 0.60) - the 30-year age gap only counts once both features are comparable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The same neighbour in both cases, because scaling never changes neighbours</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Raw: $\\sqrt{1^2+3000^2}\\approx3000$ versus $\\sqrt{30^2+500^2}\\approx501$. Scaled by the ranges (50 and 480,000): P ≈ $\\sqrt{0.02^2+0.006^2}\\approx0.021$, Q ≈ $\\sqrt{0.6^2+0.001^2}\\approx0.60$. Scaling <strong>changes the neighbour</strong>. A, B and D miss this.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The KNN slides describe lazy learning, choosing K, calculating distances and majority voting.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part D - Artificial Neural Networks</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q43</span><div><p>An artificial neuron computes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=w_1x_1+w_2x_2+b$$</div>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_1=2,\\quad x_1=3$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$w_2=-1,\\quad x_2=4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$b=1$$</div>\n<p>What is $z$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 11</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p><strong>Working - Weighted sum</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=2(3)+(-1)(4)+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6-4+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=3}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H43</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A neuron has $w_1=0.5$, $x_1=4$, $w_2=-2$, $x_2=1.5$ and $b=-1$, with a ReLU activation. What are $z$ and the output?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> $z=-2$, output 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $z=-2$, output -2</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $z=6$, output 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $z=-1$, output 0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$z=0.5(4)+(-2)(1.5)+(-1)=2-3-1=-2$, and $\\max(0,-2)=0$. B forgets that ReLU blocks negative values. C ignores the minus signs. D forgets the bias.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q44</span><div><p>The ReLU activation function is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(z)=\\max(0,z)$$</div>\n<p>What is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(-3)$$</div></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -3</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 3</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p><strong>Working - ReLU</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(-3)=\\max(0,-3)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{0}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H44</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A hidden neuron with ReLU has a negative $z$ for <strong>every</strong> training example. What is its output?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Negative values that carry useful information to the next layer</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.5 for every example</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $|z|$ for every example</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0 for every example, so the neuron contributes nothing to the next layer for these inputs</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$ReLU(z)=\\max(0,z)$ maps every negative input to 0 - that neuron is effectively switched off (and passes no gradient back through that path). A describes a linear or tanh-like activation. B is $\\sigma(0)$. C is an absolute-value function, not ReLU.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q45</span><div><p>Why are nonlinear activation functions important in multilayer neural networks?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They guarantee perfect prediction accuracy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They replace the need for training labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They remove every unnecessary feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They allow the network to represent nonlinear relationships rather than only compositions of linear transformations</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H45</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A network has three hidden layers but uses <strong>no</strong> activation function (every neuron outputs $z$ unchanged). What can it represent?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Any nonlinear function, because it has three layers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only a linear function - composing linear transformations gives another linear transformation, so it is no more expressive than one linear layer and cannot solve XOR</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only the XOR function</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Nothing - it cannot be trained</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Without <strong>nonlinear activations</strong>, stacking layers just multiplies weight matrices together. Depth alone does not add expressiveness (A). C and D are false - it trains fine, but only as a linear model.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q46</span><div><p>Why is XOR a classic example demonstrating the need for nonlinear representation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Its output classes cannot be separated by a single straight-line boundary in the original two-dimensional input space</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It has no known labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It always produces a continuous target value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It can only be solved using Random Forest</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H46</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>The lecture's XOR network uses $H_1=\\sigma(-4.8x_1+5.1x_2-2.6)$, $H_2=\\sigma(4.6x_1-5.2x_2-3.2)$ and $\\hat y=\\sigma(5.9H_1+5.2H_2-2.7)$. For the input (1, 1), what is the output?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.94 → class 1, because both inputs are active</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Exactly 0.5 → undecided</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 0.11 → class 0 - both hidden units are nearly off ($H_1\\approx0.09$, $H_2\\approx0.02$), so the output bias dominates</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 0.81 → class 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$H_1=\\sigma(-2.3)\\approx0.091$ and $H_2=\\sigma(-3.8)\\approx0.022$, so $z_Y\\approx5.9(0.091)+5.2(0.022)-2.7\\approx-2.05$ and $\\sigma(-2.05)\\approx0.11$. Each hidden unit acts as <strong>one line</strong> in input space; the output combines them into a region no single line can make. A is the output for (0, 1). D is the output for (1, 0). B has no basis.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q47</span><div><p>What is the primary role of backpropagation during neural-network training?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Remove all hidden layers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Randomly assign final class labels</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Compute gradients of the loss with respect to network parameters</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Convert input features into text</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H47</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Backpropagation gives $\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}=0.5$ for a weight $w=0.8$. With gradient descent and $\\eta=0.1$, what is the new weight, and which step did backpropagation perform?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.75 - backpropagation computed the gradient, and the update then moved the weight against it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.85 - the gradient is added to the weight</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.3 - the learning rate is not needed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.8 - backpropagation never affects the weights</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$w_{new}=0.8-0.1\\times0.5=0.75$. Backpropagation <strong>computes gradients</strong>; the optimizer uses them to update. B moves uphill. C ignores η. D is half right - backpropagation itself only computes the gradient - but the training step does change the weight.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q48</span><div><p>What is a common purpose of early stopping?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Guarantee training accuracy reaches 100%</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Stop training when validation performance no longer improves sufficiently, helping limit overfitting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Force all neural-network weights to zero</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove every nonlinear activation function</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H48</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Training and validation loss by epoch:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Epoch</th>\n<th>Training loss</th>\n<th>Validation loss</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0.40</td>\n<td>0.45</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>0.25</td>\n<td>0.32</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>0.15</td>\n<td>0.30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>0.08</td>\n<td>0.36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>0.03</td>\n<td>0.48</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which model does early stopping keep?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Epoch 50 - its training loss is lowest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Epoch 10 - the gap between the losses is smallest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Epoch 20 - training stops at the first improvement.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Epoch 30 - validation loss is lowest; after it, training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises (overfitting).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Early stopping watches <strong>validation</strong> performance and keeps the best point before it worsens. A rewards memorization. B: a small gap with high loss is underfitting. C stops too early.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<p>The neural-network section covers perceptrons, weighted sums, activation functions, hidden layers, backpropagation, XOR and overfitting.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part E - Lecture 7 Coding Questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q49</span><div><p><strong>Coding - SVM</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nmodel = SVC(\n    kernel=&quot;rbf&quot;,\n    C=1.0\n)\n\nmodel.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does kernel=\"rbf\" allow the model to do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Perform Ordinary Least Squares</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use a nonlinear kernel to model more complex decision boundaries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Automatically remove every outlier</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Convert classification into clustering</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H49</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>Compared with <code>SVC(kernel=\"linear\")</code>, which statement about <code>SVC(kernel=\"rbf\")</code> is correct?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It explicitly computes and stores every high-dimensional transformed feature.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It measures similarity through distances (the kernel trick), allowing curved boundaries in the original space without building the transformed features - which is also why it needs scaled inputs.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It turns the SVM into a regression model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It removes the need for the parameter C.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The <strong>kernel trick</strong> computes similarities as if in a higher-dimensional space, without building that space (A is the opposite). RBF is distance-based, hence <code>X_train_scaled</code>. C: SVC is still a classifier. D: C still controls the penalty for violations.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q50</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Naïve Bayes</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n\nmodel = GaussianNB()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\npredictions = model.predict(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which statement is correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> GaussianNB performs KNN distance calculations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> GaussianNB constructs 100 Decision Trees</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> GaussianNB is a probabilistic classifier that models class-conditional feature distributions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> GaussianNB uses polynomial least-squares fitting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H50</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>GaussianNB().fit(X_train, y_train)</code> is run on continuous features such as temperature and income. What does <code>fit()</code> learn?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> For each class: the prior, and the mean and variance of each feature (a normal distribution per feature per class); predictions combine these class-conditional densities with the prior.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It stores the training data and later computes distances to it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It builds an ensemble of trees.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One coefficient per feature, found by least squares.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>GaussianNB is a <strong>probabilistic classifier</strong> that models each feature's distribution within each class (normal distributions for continuous data), then applies Bayes' rule with the naïve independence assumption. B is KNN. C is a random forest. D is linear regression.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q51</span><div><p><strong>Coding - KNN</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n\nmodel = KNeighborsClassifier(\n    n_neighbors=5\n)\n\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does n_neighbors=5 specify?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Consider five nearest neighbours when making a prediction</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use exactly five target classes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Train five neural networks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove five features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H51</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p>A binary classifier uses <code>KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=4)</code>. What is the risk, and what does the lecture advise?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Even values of K are not allowed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Predictions become numerical, as in regression.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A 2-2 tie between the classes can occur; the lecture recommends an odd K for binary classification.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> An even K is always more accurate.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>With four voters and two classes, the vote can <strong>split evenly</strong>; an odd K makes a binary tie impossible (though it cannot rule out ties with more classes). A is false - the library accepts it. B and D are unrelated.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q52</span><div><p><strong>Coding - Neural Networks</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.neural_network import MLPClassifier\n\nmodel = MLPClassifier(\n    hidden_layer_sizes=(8, 4),\n    max_iter=1000,\n    random_state=42\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does hidden_layer_sizes=(8, 4) specify?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Eight input features and four output classes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Eight training observations and four test observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Four hidden layers containing eight neurons each</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Two hidden layers containing eight and four neurons respectively</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong></div></details><details class=\"harder-reveal\"><summary>Harder version <span class=\"twin-badge\">H52</span></summary><div class=\"harder-body\"><div class=\"harder-question\"><p><code>MLPClassifier(hidden_layer_sizes=(8, 4))</code> is trained on 10 input features with a binary target (one output neuron). How many connection weights, <strong>excluding biases</strong>, does the network have?</p></div><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 12 - 8 + 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 116 - 10x8 + 8x4 + 4x1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 32 - 8x4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 320 - 10x8x4</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Each layer is fully connected to the next: input→H1 has $10\\times8=80$, H1→H2 has $8\\times4=32$, and H2→output has $4\\times1=4$, giving 116. hidden_layer_sizes sets only the <strong>hidden</strong> layers; the input and output sizes come from the data. A counts neurons. C counts one layer pair. D multiplies across layers.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 7 - Answer Key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>25</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>39</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>40</td>\n<td>D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>41</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>42</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>43</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>44</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>31</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>45</td>\n<td>D</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>46</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>33</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>47</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>34</td>\n<td>D</td>\n<td>48</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>49</td>\n<td>B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>36</td>\n<td>C</td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>37</td>\n<td>B</td>\n<td>51</td>\n<td>A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>38</td>\n<td>A</td>\n<td>52</td>\n<td>D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Important calculations and explanations</h3>\n<p><strong>Q32 - Prior probability</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes)=\\frac6{10}=\\boxed{0.6}$$</div>\n<p>The prior is calculated before observing the new customer's evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Q34 - Naïve Bayes posterior</strong></p>\n<p>First calculate the unnormalized class scores:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(Yes)=0.6(0.3)=0.18$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Score(No)=0.4(0.2)=0.08$$</div>\n<p>Normalize:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(Yes\\mid X)=\\frac{0.18}{0.18+0.08}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\frac{0.18}{0.26}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{P(Yes\\mid X)\\approx0.692}$$</div>\n<p>Since Yes has the higher posterior, predict Yes.</p>\n<p>Exam trap: The raw value 0.18 is not the normalized posterior probability.</p>\n<p><strong>Q39 - Euclidean distance</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$d=\\sqrt{(3-0)^2+(4-0)^2}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{9+16}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=\\sqrt{25}=\\boxed{5}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q43 - Weighted sum</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$z=2(3)+(-1)(4)+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$=6-4+1$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{z=3}$$</div>\n<p><strong>Q44 - ReLU</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$ReLU(-3)=\\max(0,-3)$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{0}$$</div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">The biggest MCQ traps in Lectures 6-7</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>1. Logistic Regression is not ordinary numerical regression</h4>\n<p>Despite the word regression, the lecture primarily uses Logistic Regression for binary classification.</p>\n<p>Linear Regression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat y=mX+c$$</div>\n<p>Logistic Regression:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\hat p=\\frac1{1+e^{-z}}$$</div>\n<p>One produces numerical predictions; the other produces estimated probabilities used for class decisions.</p>\n<h4>2. Gini = 0 means purity</h4>\n<p>Remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=1-\\sum p_i^2$$</div>\n<p>For a completely pure node:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=0$$</div>\n<p>For a 50/50 binary node:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Gini=0.5$$</div>\n<p>Lower weighted child impurity is preferred when comparing candidate tree splits, subject to the model's constraints.</p>\n<h4>3. Random Forest reduces variance; it does not eliminate overfitting</h4>\n<p>The benefit of combining multiple randomized trees is that their errors may partially offset one another.</p>\n<p>But Random Forest is not guaranteed to outperform every single tree on every dataset.</p>\n<h4>4. Large C in SVM does not mean a larger margin</h4>\n<p>In a soft-margin SVM, larger C penalizes violations more strongly.</p>\n<p>That can result in a narrower margin.</p>\n<p>A smaller C allows a greater trade-off in favour of regularization.</p>\n<p>The lecture's hard-margin illustrations are useful, but in practical soft-margin SVM, some margin violations or misclassifications may be accepted.</p>\n<h4>5. Naïve Bayes assumes conditional independence</h4>\n<p>More precisely:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P(X_1,\\ldots,X_n\\mid C)\n=\n\\prod_iP(X_i\\mid C)$$</div>\n<p>The assumption is about feature independence given the class, not necessarily unconditional independence in the overall population.</p>\n<h4>6. Small K vs large K</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Small K</th>\n<th>Large K</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Highly local predictions</td>\n<td>Smoother predictions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greater noise sensitivity</td>\n<td>Greater risk of underfitting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can overfit</td>\n<td>Can ignore local structure</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Neither choice is universally better. Select K using validation.</p>\n<h4>7. Neural networks need nonlinear activation functions</h4>\n<p>Without nonlinear activation functions, stacking affine layers still gives an affine transformation.</p>\n<p>This is why XOR cannot be solved using a single linear separating boundary but can be represented using an appropriately constructed neural network with hidden layers and nonlinear activations.</p>\n<h4>8. Don't memorize every neural-network weight as zero</h4>\n<p>The lecture's later correction on the slides clarifies that initializing all hidden-layer weights identically can create a symmetry problem. Modern multilayer neural networks generally use suitable randomized weight initialization.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Final Rapid-Fire Revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Known inputs and outputs</td>\n<td>Supervised learning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict house price</td>\n<td>Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict Fraud/Legitimate</td>\n<td>Classification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best-fitting straight line</td>\n<td>Linear Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimize squared residuals</td>\n<td>OLS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple predictors</td>\n<td>Multiple Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Curved relationship using $X^2$</td>\n<td>Polynomial Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sigmoid class probabilities</td>\n<td>Logistic Regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision rules</td>\n<td>Decision Tree</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pure classification node</td>\n<td>Gini = 0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many randomized trees</td>\n<td>Random Forest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Majority vote</td>\n<td>Random Forest classification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Average tree predictions</td>\n<td>Random Forest regression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum-margin separation</td>\n<td>SVM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Critical observations determining margin</td>\n<td>Support vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonlinear separation</td>\n<td>Kernel SVM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bayesian posterior probability</td>\n<td>Naïve Bayes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zero-frequency correction</td>\n<td>Laplace smoothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Similar stored observations</td>\n<td>KNN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KNN with one neighbour</td>\n<td>Highly local, noise-sensitive</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weighted inputs and bias</td>\n<td>Artificial neuron</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonlinearity</td>\n<td>Activation functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative input becomes zero</td>\n<td>ReLU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not linearly separable</td>\n<td>XOR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gradients propagated backward</td>\n<td>Backpropagation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training stops based on validation</td>\n<td>Early stopping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l7p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">What I would prioritize before the exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>For Lecture 6, focus most on regression calculations (prediction, residual, SSE), the sigmoid function, Gini impurity, Decision Tree structure and Random Forest voting versus averaging.</p>\n<p>For Lecture 7, focus on SVM margin and support vectors, Bayes' Theorem calculations, Naïve Bayes independence, KNN distances and the effect of K, followed by neural-network weighted sums, activation functions and backpropagation.</p>\n<p>The highest-value skill is being able to identify how each algorithm learns, rather than simply recognizing its name. That's what allows you to solve scenario MCQs where several algorithms initially appear plausible.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l7-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Bloom challenge - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 7 Bloom challenge</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"7\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which pairing of algorithm and core idea matches the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> SVM → probability; Naïve Bayes → similarity; KNN → separation; neural networks → rules</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> SVM → separation; Naïve Bayes → probability; KNN → similarity; neural networks → learned transformations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> SVM → similarity; Naïve Bayes → learned transformations; KNN → probability; neural networks → separation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All four rely on decision rules.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture frames Lecture 7 around <strong>separation, probability, similarity and learned transformations</strong>. A and C shuffle the ideas. D describes trees from Lecture 6.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which output ranges are correct?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sigmoid $[0,\\infty)$; tanh $(0,1)$; ReLU $(-1,1)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sigmoid $(-1,1)$; tanh $[0,\\infty)$; ReLU $(0,1)$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All three output values between 0 and 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sigmoid $(0,1)$; tanh $(-1,1)$; ReLU $[0,\\infty)$</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The <strong>sigmoid</strong> squashes to probabilities, <strong>tanh</strong> is the zero-centred version, and <strong>ReLU</strong> passes positive values unchanged and zeroes negative ones. A and B shuffle the ranges. C is true only for the sigmoid.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What is the order of the training cycle described in the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Initialize → Forward pass → Calculate loss → Backpropagate → Update weights → repeat</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Forward pass → Update weights → Initialize → Calculate loss → Backpropagate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Backpropagate → Initialize → Forward pass → Update weights → Calculate loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Initialize → Update weights → Forward pass → Backpropagate → Calculate loss</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>You need a prediction (<strong>forward pass</strong>) before you can measure error (<strong>loss</strong>); you need the loss before you can compute <strong>gradients</strong>; and the gradients drive the <strong>update</strong>. B, C and D break that dependency chain.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Words in an email are clearly not independent, yet Naïve Bayes often works well for spam filtering. Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Spam words really are independent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Naïve Bayes stops multiplying when features are dependent.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Classification only needs the correct class to get the <strong>highest</strong> score, which often survives a wrong independence assumption even when the probabilities themselves are poorly calibrated; the method is also fast and copes with many features.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Naïve Bayes uses distances, so independence does not matter.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The assumption distorts the <strong>probability values</strong>, but the ranking of the classes is often still right - the lecture's \"poorly calibrated probabilities\" caveat. A is false. B is not how the method works. D describes KNN.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why shouldn't every weight in a hidden layer start at zero?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Zero weights cause division by zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Every neuron in the layer then computes the same output and receives the same gradient, so they stay identical and the layer cannot learn different features - the symmetry problem.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Zero weights make the network linear forever.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Zero is not a valid floating-point value.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Identical starting points plus identical updates keep the neurons as <strong>clones</strong>. Small random values (Xavier/Glorot for sigmoid/tanh, He for ReLU) break the symmetry. The lecture corrects its early slide on this point. A and D are false. C confuses this with the missing-activation problem.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What does the kernel trick let an SVM do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Separate classes with a straight line in the original input space, whatever the data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Remove the need for support vectors.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Turn classification into regression.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compute similarities as if the data had been mapped into a higher-dimensional space, without explicitly building every transformed feature, so curved boundaries become possible.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Some data (e.g. concentric circles) becomes separable after mapping to more dimensions; a kernel gives the <strong>inner products in that space</strong> directly. A is impossible for such data. B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A KNN regressor with $K=3$ finds neighbours whose house prices are RM200k, RM220k and RM260k. What does it predict, using $\\hat y=\\frac1K\\sum y_i$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> RM220k, the median</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> RM260k, the nearest</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> RM680k</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About RM226.7k</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>$(200+220+260)/3\\approx226.7$. KNN <strong>regression averages</strong> the neighbours' targets; classification votes. A uses the median. B assumes the order of the list is the order of distance. C forgets to divide by K.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Buy = Yes has 4 training rows and Buy = No has 6. Weather has $K=3$ values. \"Sunny\" appears 0 times among Yes rows and 3 times among No rows. With add-one smoothing and only this feature, what is $P(Yes\\mid Sunny)$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0 - Yes never saw Sunny</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 0.176</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0.4, the prior</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>$P(Sunny\\mid Yes)=\\frac{0+1}{4+3}=\\frac17$ and $P(Sunny\\mid No)=\\frac{3+1}{6+3}=\\frac49$. Scores: $0.4\\times\\frac17\\approx0.0571$ and $0.6\\times\\frac49\\approx0.2667$. Normalized: $0.0571/0.3238\\approx0.176$. A is the unsmoothed zero-frequency result. C ignores the evidence. D has no basis.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A neuron has $w_1=0.5, x_1=2, w_2=-1, x_2=1, b=0.2$ and a sigmoid activation. The true target is 1. What are the output and the squared-error loss $(y-\\hat y)^2$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Output ≈ 0.55, loss ≈ 0.20</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Output 0.2, loss 0.64</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Output ≈ 0.45, loss ≈ 0.30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Output 1, loss 0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$z=1-1+0.2=0.2$; $\\sigma(0.2)\\approx0.550$; loss $(1-0.55)^2\\approx0.203$. B forgets the activation. C uses $\\sigma(-0.2)$. D treats the output as already correct. This loss drives backpropagation.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Validation error for a KNN classifier: K = 1 → 20%, K = 7 → 12%, K = 51 → 25%. What is the best analysis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> K = 1 is best, because it uses the closest neighbour.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Larger K is always better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> K = 1 is too sensitive to noisy individual points (high variance), K = 51 smooths away local structure (underfitting), and K = 7 balances the two.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KNN's error does not depend on K.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>K controls <strong>flexibility</strong>: small K gives jagged boundaries, large K gives very smooth ones. The validation minimum picks the trade-off. A and B are the two extremes the lecture warns about. D contradicts the numbers.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Three training runs: run P's loss jumps up and down and grows over time; run Q's loss falls steadily but extremely slowly; run R's loss falls quickly and then levels off. What is the most likely diagnosis?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> P: learning rate too large (overshooting); Q: too small; R: a reasonable learning rate</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> P: too small; Q: too large; R: too large</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All three show overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The learning rate cannot affect the loss curve.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The learning rate sets the <strong>step size</strong> in $w_{new}=w_{old}-\\eta\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}$: too big overshoots and can diverge, too small crawls. B reverses it. C: overfitting is diagnosed with validation loss, not with these training curves. D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>An RBF SVM gives: C = 1000 → train 100%, test 82%; C = 1 → train 94%, test 91%. What explains this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Large C weakens the penalty on violations, so the model underfits.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> C = 1000 must have used a different kernel.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The results show that C has no effect.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Large C penalizes violations heavily, so the boundary bends to classify every training point, including noise - a narrow margin and overfitting; C = 1 tolerates some violations and generalizes better.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>C trades <strong>training fit against margin width</strong>. A 100% / 82% split is the overfitting signature. A reverses what C does. B and C ignore the evidence.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A payments app must score 10 million stored transactions' worth of history and answer each new request within 1 ms. Which assessment of KNN is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KNN is ideal, because training is instant.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KNN is ideal, because it needs no tuning.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KNN is a poor fit as-is: as a lazy learner it computes distances to the stored data at prediction time, which is costly at this scale; a model that does its work during training (e.g. logistic regression, a tree ensemble, a small neural network) better suits the latency budget.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KNN is impossible on numerical features.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The lecture's cautions for KNN include <strong>slow prediction on large datasets</strong>. A ignores where the cost falls. B is false (K, the metric and scaling all need choosing). D is false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>With 2,000 labelled emails and a vocabulary of 20,000 words, a team compares Multinomial Naïve Bayes with a deep neural network. Which evaluation is most reasonable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The neural network must win, because it is more complex.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Naïve Bayes cannot handle text.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They will perform identically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Naïve Bayes is a strong, fast, low-variance baseline for small text datasets; the network might win with far more data, so compare both with cross-validation before paying for the complex model.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>The lecture lists NB as <strong>efficient and useful for spam filtering and document classification</strong>, and few labels favour simple models. A confuses capacity with performance. B is false. C has no basis.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A network's training loss keeps falling while its validation loss has risen for 30 epochs. Which response is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keep training - the training loss is still improving.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Use early stopping at the validation minimum, and consider dropout and more representative training data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Increase the learning rate tenfold.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Remove the validation set.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Falling training loss with rising validation loss is <strong>overfitting</strong> - the lecture's page-101 pattern - and the listed remedies are early stopping, dropout and more data. A makes it worse. C fixes nothing. D just hides the problem.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a KNN classifier for customer data with age, income and tenure.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scale\", StandardScaler()), (\"knn\", KNeighborsClassifier())])</code> with GridSearchCV over odd <code>n_neighbors</code> (e.g. 3-31) on the training set, then a final test-set check.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KNN on raw features with K = 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Scale with statistics from the whole dataset, then pick K using test accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> K = the training-set size.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>It combines <strong>scaling</strong> (distances), <strong>choosing K by validation</strong> (bias-variance), <strong>odd K</strong> (no binary ties) and a <strong>pipeline</strong> (no leakage). B lets income dominate and overfits. C leaks in two ways. D always predicts the majority class.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design an SVM for a two-class dataset with a clearly curved boundary.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>SVC(kernel=\"linear\")</code> on raw features with C = 10⁶.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Linear regression thresholded at 0.5.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>Pipeline([(\"scale\", StandardScaler()), (\"svm\", SVC(kernel=\"rbf\"))])</code>, tuning <code>C</code> and <code>gamma</code> together by cross-validation, then evaluating on a held-out test set.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>SVC(kernel=\"rbf\")</code> with C and gamma chosen to maximise training accuracy.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>An RBF kernel handles <strong>non-linear</strong> boundaries; it is distance-based, so scaling is needed; and C and gamma jointly control flexibility, so they are tuned together by validation. A can't bend and overfits whatever it can. B isn't a classifier designed for this. D invites overfitting.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design a neural network for tabular data with an XOR-like interaction between two features.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A network with no hidden layer.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Scaled inputs; at least one hidden layer with a non-linear activation (e.g. ReLU) and small random initial weights (He initialization); a sigmoid output with cross-entropy loss; a sensible learning rate; early stopping on a validation split.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Several hidden layers with no activation functions, all weights initialized to zero.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A single neuron with a step activation.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>XOR needs a <strong>hidden layer plus a non-linearity</strong>; random initialization breaks symmetry; and validation-based early stopping limits overfitting. A and D are linear separators, which cannot solve XOR. C is linear and stuck by symmetry.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A soft-margin SVM minimizes the hinge loss $\\max(0,\\,1-y\\,f(x))$ plus a margin penalty. For $y=+1$, what is the loss when $f(x)=0.4$ and when $f(x)=1.5$, and why does that matter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.6 and 0 - points beyond the margin ($y f(x)\\ge1$) contribute nothing, so only points on or inside the margin (the support vectors) shape the solution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.6 and 1.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0 and 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0.4 and 1.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$1-0.4=0.6$, and $\\max(0,1-1.5)=0$. Zero loss for comfortably correct points is why the solution depends only on the <strong>support vectors</strong>, and in the dual problem only they get non-zero weights $\\alpha_i$. C scales the hinge penalty against the margin term, which is the lecture's \"C controls the trade-off\".</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>What does Mercer's condition require of a function to be a valid SVM kernel?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It must be linear.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It must output values between 0 and 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It must use Euclidean distance.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> For any set of points, its matrix of pairwise values (the Gram matrix) must be symmetric and positive semi-definite, so that it equals an inner product in some feature space.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Positive semi-definiteness guarantees a feature map $\\phi$ with $K(x,x')=\\phi(x)\\cdot\\phi(x')$, and keeps the SVM optimization convex. That is what makes the <strong>kernel trick</strong> legitimate. RBF, polynomial and linear kernels all qualify. A, B and C are not required (the polynomial kernel is not bounded, for example).</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>For a linear SVM with $w=(3,4)$, the margin boundaries are $w\\cdot x+b=\\pm1$. What is the width of the margin?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 7</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 2</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The width is $\\frac{2}{\\lVert w\\rVert}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}}=\\frac25=0.4$. So <strong>maximizing the margin</strong> means minimizing $\\lVert w\\rVert$ - the objective SVM optimizes. C is $\\lVert w\\rVert$. A sums the components. D forgets to divide by $\\lVert w\\rVert$.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>The RBF kernel is $K(x,x')=\\exp(-\\gamma\\lVert x-x'\\rVert^2)$. Two points have $\\lVert x-x'\\rVert^2=2$. What is the similarity for $\\gamma=0.1$ and for $\\gamma=10$, and what does a large γ do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.2 and 20; a large γ widens the boundary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both 1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 0.82 and $2\\times10^{-9}$; a large γ makes each point's influence very local, giving wiggly boundaries that can overfit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 0.82 and 0.82</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$e^{-0.2}\\approx0.819$ and $e^{-20}\\approx2.1\\times10^{-9}$. γ sets the <strong>reach</strong> of each training point, much like a small K in KNN. scikit-learn's default <code>gamma=\"scale\"</code> uses $1/(n_{features}\\cdot\\operatorname{Var}(X))$, which is one more reason scaling matters. A forgets the exponential.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A product team needs probabilities from an <code>SVC</code>. What does <code>SVC(probability=True)</code> do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Returns the raw decision values as probabilities.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Makes the SVM unsupervised.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Switches to Naïve Bayes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fits a sigmoid to the SVM's decision values using internal cross-validation (Platt scaling) - slower training, and <code>predict_proba</code> can occasionally disagree with <code>predict</code>.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>SVMs output <strong>distances to the boundary</strong>, not probabilities; Platt scaling maps them through a fitted logistic curve (5-fold CV inside scikit-learn). That is the same sigmoid as in logistic regression, used for calibration. A, B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>A Naïve Bayes text model multiplies 500 likelihoods of about 0.01 each. What happens in float64, and what is the fix?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The product $10^{-1000}$ underflows to exactly 0 for every class; sum log-probabilities instead ($500\\ln0.01\\approx-2302.6$), which compares classes safely.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The result is exact, because float64 has unlimited range.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The product becomes 1.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Use float32 for more range.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>float64 cannot represent numbers below about $10^{-308}$ (or $5\\times10^{-324}$ as subnormals), so every class score becomes 0 and cannot be compared. Logs turn the product into a <strong>sum</strong>, and the argmax is unchanged. scikit-learn works with joint log-likelihoods internally. B is false. D makes it worse.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>GaussianNB models a feature for class Yes as normal with mean 20 and SD 2. What is the likelihood $f(22\\mid Yes)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,\\sigma}e^{-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 0.5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.6065</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> About 0.121</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>$\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\approx0.1995$, times $e^{-4/8}=e^{-0.5}\\approx0.6065$, gives ≈ 0.121. B forgets the normalizing factor. It is a <strong>density</strong>, not a probability - with a tiny σ it can exceed 1. It is the continuous counterpart of the lecture's frequency counts.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>With K = 3, the neighbours are No at distance 1, Yes at distance 4 and Yes at distance 5. What do uniform voting and <code>weights=\"distance\"</code> (weight $1/d$) predict?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both Yes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Uniform: Yes (2 vs 1); distance-weighted: No (1.0 vs 0.25 + 0.2 = 0.45)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Both No</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Uniform: No; distance-weighted: Yes</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Uniform voting counts heads. Weighting by $1/d$ lets the very close neighbour outweigh the two distant ones. It is a practical refinement of the lecture's majority vote, and it also softens the impact of the choice of K. D reverses both results.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>A single neuron has $\\hat y=\\sigma(wx+b)$ with $x=2$, $w=0.5$, $b=-1$, target $y=1$ and loss $L=\\tfrac12(\\hat y-y)^2$. Using the chain rule $\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}=(\\hat y-y)\\,\\hat y(1-\\hat y)\\,x$, what is the gradient, and what is the new $w$ with $\\eta=0.1$?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> -0.25; $w=0.525$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 0.25; $w=0.475$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> -0.5; $w=0.55$</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 0; $w$ stays 0.5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$z=0$, so $\\hat y=0.5$ and $\\sigma'=\\hat y(1-\\hat y)=0.25$. The gradient is $(-0.5)(0.25)(2)=-0.25$, and $w=0.5-0.1(-0.25)=0.525$. <strong>Backpropagation</strong> is this chain rule applied layer by layer. B gets the sign wrong. C forgets the sigmoid's derivative.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>The sigmoid's derivative is at most 0.25. In a 10-layer sigmoid network, the gradient reaching the first layer includes a product of 10 such factors. What does this imply?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Early layers learn fastest.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The gradient can shrink by a factor of up to $0.25^{10}\\approx10^{-6}$, so early layers barely update (vanishing gradients). ReLU-family activations with He initialization, normalization layers or residual connections ease this.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Gradients always explode with sigmoid.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Depth does not affect gradients.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Backpropagation <strong>multiplies</strong> local derivatives; many factors ≤ 0.25 drive the product towards zero. That is why the lecture pairs ReLU with He initialization and sigmoid/tanh with Xavier (which keeps the variance of the signal stable across layers). A is backwards. C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>To speed up KNN, a team considers a KD-tree. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> KD-trees make KNN exact and fast in any number of dimensions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> KD-trees remove the need for scaling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> KD-trees turn KNN into a decision tree classifier.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> KD-trees (and ball trees) prune the search well in low dimensions, but in high dimensions they degrade towards brute force; at large scale, approximate nearest-neighbour indexes (e.g. HNSW or FAISS) trade a little accuracy for big speed-ups.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Brute-force KNN costs $O(n\\cdot d)$ per query - the lecture's lazy-learning drawback. Space partitioning helps only while the dimension is small, which links to the curse of dimensionality. scikit-learn's <code>algorithm=\"auto\"</code> chooses for you. A, B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>\"The universal approximation theorem shows a network with one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function, so one hidden layer is all we ever need.\" Which evaluation is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct - depth is never useful.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The theorem is false.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The theorem (Cybenko 1989; Hornik 1991) guarantees that a good approximation <strong>exists</strong> given enough hidden units, but not how many units it takes (possibly exponentially many), that gradient descent will find it, or that it will generalize from finite data; deeper networks often represent the same functions far more efficiently.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It applies only to linear activations.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Existence is not learnability or generalization. Overfitting, initialization and optimization still decide what happens in practice, which ties back to the lecture's training and overfitting sections. A over-reads the theorem. B is false. D is wrong: the theorem needs a <strong>non-linear</strong> activation.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
